# Identical to packaging/aur/vanta/vanta.install apart from the filename:
# the note is about the engine Vanta runs, not about whichever package or
# build supplied it, so it must not drift between the three packages. Said
# here as well as in the pkgdesc, because a package registry is where a false
# promise costs most: someone installs, launches, gets a dialog they did not
# expect, and files a bug against a maintainer rather than reading a README.

_note() {
  cat <<'MSG'

Vanta ships no Roblox build. On first run it offers to fetch one, and
installs it only if Roblox's own signing certificate signed it.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the build comes from a third-party mirror,
and Vanta keeps its own copy of it. If you would rather use Sober's build, or
an APK you already have, Settings > Roblox can copy either in. Vanta does not
follow Sober's updates after that.

Start it with `vanta-shell`, or from your application list. `vanta-run` is
the loader the shell launches and is not what you run by hand.

Vanta is early. You can sign in, load an experience, play with keyboard and
mouse, type into text fields and hear sound. Not working: voice chat, and on
roughly one launch in three a signed-in client reaches the home screen and
freezes -- reopening usually works.
https://github.com/gpt4omni/vanta#readme says which claims were measured and
how.

MSG
}

post_install() {
  _note
}

post_upgrade() {
  :
}